Some tips for and changes to the stock Debix image (current is Ubuntu 22.04)
'$' means execute command in the shell. 

* OLED display example is in ~/src/oled-logo-set. Symlink to ~/bin so it's in the PATH after a new login
  $ ln -s ~/src/oled-logo-set/oled-logo-set ~/bin

* Copy or symlink the scripts from src/scripts to ~/bin
  $ ln -s ~/ase2024-bootlogo.sh ~/bin
  $ ln -s ~/ase_shutdown.sh 	~/bin
  $ ln -s ~/ase_startup.sh 	~/bin
  $ ln -s ~/hc12-setup.sh 	~/bin
  $ ln -s ~/show-devicetree.sh 	~/bin

* Allow any user in the sudo group to execute sudo without the password prompt.
- $ sudo visudo                 # Don't edit the /etc/sudoers file directly!
- Comment out the line with the sudo group: "# %sudo  ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL"
- Add "%sudo   ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL"

* Update packages
  $ sudo apt-update && sudo apt upgrade

* Run unminimize, it will restore the 'normal' linux environment (manpages etc)
  $ sudo unminimize

* Install extra packages
  $ sudo apt install vim command-not-found apt-file i2c-tools libi2c-dev less picocom vim build-essential mpg123

* Change hostname in /etc/hostname
- /etc/hostname -> debix-nelis

* For a prettier serial console, add the following to ~/.bashrc, right before the if[$color_prompt] block (about line 59)
  # NA - set term type if we're on the serial console 
  if [ "$(tty)" == "/dev/ttymxc1" ]; then
      export TERM=xterm
      color_prompt=yes
      stty rows 40 columns 132
  fi
  ...if [ "$color_prompt" = yes ]; then

* You can also set the preferred editor in .bashrc. 
 export EDITOR=nano"
  If you change your mind, use "$ export EDITOR=vim"

* Add the following to /etc/rc.local, above the "exit 0". This will run that script at boot
  if [ -e /home/debix/bin/ase_startup.sh ]
  then
          /home/debix/bin/ase_startup.sh
  fi

* Copy the ase2024-bootlogo service script to the systemd location and enable it at boot
  $ sudo cp /home/debix/src/systemd-scripts/ase2024-bootlogo.service /etc/systemd/system
  $ sudo systemctl enable ase2024-bootlogo

* Copy the ase_shutdown.sh script to /lib/systemd/system-shutdown/ 
  $ sudo cp /home/debix/bin /lib/systemd/system-shutdown/ 

* Save a lot of CPU cycles by disabling the graphical login:
- Disable graphical boot: 
  $ sudo systemctl set-default multi-user.target
- To re-enable graphical boot: 
  $ sudo systemctl set-default graphical.target

* Disable some services 
  $ sudo systemctl disable ModemManager		(may interfere with serial ports)
  $ sudo systemctl status imx8-isp.service	(floods log with errors about IMX219 xclk and we don't use that camera anyway)

* BNO055 sysfs config (also see https://lwn.net/ml/linux-kernel/20220613120534.36991-9-andrea.merello@iit.it/)
  - Module is in /lib/modules/6.1.22-ase2024/kernel/drivers/iio/imu
  $ sudo modprobe bno055_i2c
  $ echo bno055_i2c 0x28 | sudo tee /sys/bus/i2c/devices/i2c-5/new_device	# register
  $ echo 0x28 | sudo tee /sys/bus/i2c/devices/i2c-5/delete_device		# unregister
  - TODO: Where is the sysfs interface?
  - Also not detected by lsiio (from kernelsource/tools/iio)

modprobe mt7921u
echo 0846 9060 > /sys/bus/usb/drivers/mt7921u/new_id
/lib/firmware/mediatek
